{"custom_id":"s-ap_statistics-m11-k0","ok":true,"model":"claude-opus-5-5","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfypEzvF4sA2q22Hxq3hk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Changing from n = 40, α = 0.05 to n = 30, α = 0.1 raises both the Type I error probability and the power (choice D, index 3).\n\n1. **Type I error:** The probability of a Type I error equals α. Raising α from 0.05 to 0.1 increases it.\n2. **Power:** From the table, power at n = 40, α = 0.05 is 0.47. At n = 30, α = 0.1 it is 0.53, so power increases. The bigger α outweighs the loss from the smaller sample.\n\nI checked the table with a z-test (σ = 80, true mean 520 vs. 500):\n- For n = 40, the standard error is 80/√40 ≈ 12.65. Power = P(Z > 1.645 − 20/12.65) ≈ 0.474.\n- For n = 30, the standard error is 80/√30 ≈ 14.61. Power = P(Z > 1.282 − 20/14.61) ≈ 0.535.\n\nBoth match the table, and the item is not flawed."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HiiMsb9dMB9F3J345VoEhW","name":"submit_solution","input":{"answers":[{"item":"1","choice_index":3,"final_answer":"Type I error probability increases (alpha 0.05 to 0.1) and power increases (0.47 to 0.53)","brief_reasoning":"P(Type I) = alpha, which rises from 0.05 to 0.1. From the table, power goes from 0.47 to 0.53. Both values check out with z-test calculations.","ambiguous_or_flawed":false}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":591,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":696,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":169},"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null},"usage":{"input_tokens":591,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_t